    <string name="how_to_play">How to play?</string>
    <string name="tutorial_offer_title">First time playing %1$s?</string>
    <string name="tutorial_offer_body">A short tutorial shows the rules on an example in a couple of minutes. You can always open it later with “How to play?”.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_offer_open">Tutorial</string>
    <string name="tutorial_offer_skip">Play</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_one_title">No three in a row</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_one_body">First pick a black or white piece below, then tap a cell. Two white pieces are already side by side: place the piece that avoids three in a row.</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_two_title">Keep the balance</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_two_body">Every row and column has as many white pieces as black. Pick the right one and fill the last cell of the row.</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_three_title">Lines never repeat</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_three_body">Finished rows and columns must differ. Place the piece that keeps the second row from repeating the top one.</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_four_title">A small puzzle of your own</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_stage_four_body">Solve the board yourself with all three rules. A correct piece locks in, a wrong one is highlighted and can be fixed. After three mistakes the board starts over. The pencil adds rough notes that never count as mistakes.</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_try_again">Not quite. Check the rule and try again.</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_complete_title">Tutorial complete</string>
    <string name="balance_tutorial_complete_body">Great! Now pick a difficulty and start playing.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_one_title">One crown per row and column</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_one_body">Pick “Crown” below and tap the highlighted cell. Every row and column has exactly one crown.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_two_title">One crown per region</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_two_body">Each region also has exactly one crown. The top-right region has two cells, but the upper one already shares a row with a crown — place the crown in the highlighted cell.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_three_title">Crowns never touch diagonally</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_three_body">Crowns cannot touch each other, not even diagonally. Place a crown in the highlighted cell: it touches none of the crowns already placed.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_four_title">Marks and pencil</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_four_body">Pick “Mark ×” and mark the highlighted cell — that is how you note there is no crown here. The pencil adds rough notes that are never checked.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_five_title">A small puzzle of your own</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_stage_five_body">Place four crowns yourself. A wrong value is a mistake, and after three mistakes the board starts over. Marks and the pencil help rule out cells.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_feedback_row_column">Not quite. This step needs a crown in the highlighted cell: two crowns in one row or column conflict.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_feedback_region">Not quite. Check the region border: it can hold only one crown.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_feedback_diagonal">Not quite. Crowns in diagonally adjacent cells conflict.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_feedback_marks">Not quite. Pick the “Mark ×” tool and tap the highlighted cell.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_feedback_mini_puzzle">There is a conflict here. Check the row, column, region, and neighbouring diagonals.</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_complete_title">Tutorial complete</string>
    <string name="crowns_tutorial_complete_body">Great! Now pick a difficulty and start a regular game.</string>
    <string name="word_rules_intro">A Russian word is hidden. The rules are simple:</string>
    <string name="word_rule_length">• The word length depends on difficulty: four to seven letters.</string>
    <string name="word_rule_attempts">• You have six tries to guess it.</string>
    <string name="word_rule_correct">• A letter in the right place is filled in.</string>
    <string name="word_rule_present">• A letter in the word but in another place gets a frame.</string>
    <string name="word_rule_absent">• A letter not in the word fades out.</string>
    <string name="word_rule_repeats">• Repeated letters count separately: an extra repeat shows as absent.</string>
    <string name="word_tutorial_example_title">Reading a try</string>
    <string name="word_tutorial_example_body">The hidden word is «ПОЛКА», the try is «ЛАМПА». «Л» and «П» are in the word but out of place, so they get a frame. «М» is not in the word. The last «А» is in place, so it is filled, and the first «А» fades: the hidden word has only one «А».</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_rules_title">Numbers without repeats</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_rules_body">Use the numbers 1–9. A number can appear only once in every row, every column, and every 3×3 block.</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_input_title">Cell first, then number</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_input_body">Pick an empty cell and enter a number. Correct numbers lock in, wrong ones count as mistakes.</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_pencil_title">Rough candidates</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_pencil_body">Turn on the pencil, pick an empty cell, and add a possible number. Notes are never checked.</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_hint_title">Mistakes and hints</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_hint_body">Three mistakes end the attempt. A hint finds the next logical move and fills in the cell — tap it. Here it is free; in the game hints come from your stock.</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_swipe_title">Swipe in four directions</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_swipe_body">Swipe left, right, up, or down — on a computer you can press the arrow keys. All tiles slide to the edge or to another tile. Try it on the board below.</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_merge_title">Merge equal tiles</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_merge_body">Two equal tiles become one with double the value, and that scores points. A tile merges at most once per move. Slide the row of twos to the left.</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_merge_before">2  2  2  2</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_merge_after">4  4</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_goal_title">Score points</string>
    <string name="game_2048_tutorial_goal_body">The level target depends on difficulty: from 12&#160;000 to 250&#160;000 points. In catalog levels the target clears the level at once, and the board stays so you can play on for a record. If you run out of moves before the target, the level is not cleared. In the daily puzzle the final score decides once no moves are left.</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_input_example">For example, only %1$d fits in the highlighted cell.</string>
    <string name="sudoku_tutorial_failed">Three mistakes — the board has started over. Try again.</string>
